    "known_incident_recovery_top1_correct": 4,
    "unknown_incident_abstention_correct": false,
    "main_findings": [
      "Semantic retrieval correctly identified relevant root causes and recovery actions for several known incidents, including Missing File, Network Path, Terminal Session, and Timeout.",
      "Semantic retrieval performed poorly on the exact UI Selector case, retrieving related but incorrect knowledge.",
      "The paraphrased UI Selector case demonstrated partial robustness because semantic retrieval still returned UI/application-related knowledge even though structured classification failed, but the retrieved results were not sufficiently specific.",
      "The unknown incident exposed a major false-positive problem: unrestricted semantic retrieval always returns nearest neighbours even when none are sufficiently relevant.",
      "Semantic similarity alone is therefore useful but insufficient as a standalone retrieval strategy for reliable RCA."
    ]
  },
  "limitations": [
    "The evaluation uses a small manually constructed incident set.",
    "The knowledge base is relatively small and manually authored.",
    "Similarity scores are retrieval scores, not calibrated probabilities.",
    "No similarity threshold or abstention mechanism was applied in this first experiment.",
    "The experiment evaluates retrieval independently of LLM-generated RCA quality.",
    "The embedding model was not tuned specifically for automation failure logs."
  ],
  "next_experiment": {
    "description": "Evaluate iterative RCA verification while preserving the current semantic retrieval results as a frozen experiment artifact.",
    "reason": "The semantic retrieval experiment establishes both useful retrieval improvements and important failure modes that should be preserved before evaluating the LLM verification loop."
  }
